OSD_Chronometer: no <sys/times.h> and no times() on wasm32-wasip1.

wasi-libc's <sys/times.h> is an #error unless -D_WASI_EMULATED_PROCESS_CLOCKS is passed, so the
include at :27 has to go away rather than be satisfied: the emulation that define unlocks measures
wall clock, not CPU time, which is the wrong answer reported as the right one. The include is
therefore guarded, and so is every use of the type and function it supplies. Nothing in the build
passes _WASI_EMULATED_PROCESS_CLOCKS, and nothing should.

The condition in force at the include is the file-wide `#ifndef _WIN32`, and it cannot be widened:
excluding WASI from it sends WASI into the Windows branch below. So both guards are new, and both
spell the same test, `#ifndef __wasi__`.

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH RETURNS: GetProcessCPU() sets both out-parameters to 0.0 and returns. That is
what GetThreadCPU() in this same file already does for a platform it cannot serve, and it is safe
for every caller because all three in-tree consumers only subtract and accumulate these values
(OSD_Chronometer::Start/Stop/Show keep a start value and a running total; Message_AttributeMeter
records them as a metric). Nothing divides by them, ranges them, or asserts on them, so a constant
zero reads as "no CPU time elapsed" and no arithmetic misbehaves.

Issue: https://github.com/SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift/issues/2179

--- a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/OSD/OSD_Chronometer.cxx
+++ b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/OSD/OSD_Chronometer.cxx
@@ -24,7 +24,9 @@
 
 #ifndef _WIN32
 
-  #include <sys/times.h>
+  #ifndef __wasi__
+    #include <sys/times.h>
+  #endif
   #include <unistd.h>
 
   #ifdef SOLARIS
@@ -52,18 +54,22 @@
 
 void OSD_Chronometer::GetProcessCPU(double& theUserSeconds, double& theSystemSeconds)
 {
-  #if defined(__linux__) || defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__ANDROID__) || defined(__QNX__)       \
-    || defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
-  static const long aCLK_TCK = sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK);
-  #else
+  theUserSeconds = theSystemSeconds = 0.0;
+  // WASI has no process-associated clocks, so CPU time is reported as zero
+  #ifndef __wasi__
+    #if defined(__linux__) || defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__ANDROID__) || defined(__QNX__)     \
+      || defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+  static const long aCLK_TCK = sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK);
+    #else
   static const long aCLK_TCK = CLK_TCK;
-  #endif
+    #endif
 
   tms aCurrentTMS{};
   times(&aCurrentTMS);
 
   theUserSeconds   = (double)aCurrentTMS.tms_utime / aCLK_TCK;
   theSystemSeconds = (double)aCurrentTMS.tms_stime / aCLK_TCK;
+  #endif
 }
 
